(meta (title "Writing in samizdat")
      (date "2026-09-26")
      (tags samizdat bjolang writing))

This is a post written in @em{samizdat}, a markup after Racket's
Scribble.@note{Scribble is at @link["https://docs.racket-lang.org/scribble/"].
It does much more than this: it is a whole language.} A command is an
@code{@"@"} and a name, and may have arguments in brackets and a body in braces.
Everything else is text, and a blank line starts a new paragraph.

@toc

@section{Why another markup}

Markdown is fine until you want something it does not have. Here, a command
is a function in @bjolang, so a blog that wants a new kind of box writes one
--- like the aside below --- and puts it in its table of commands.

@aside{
  An aside is not a standard command. @code{examples/render.bjo} defines it,
  and tells the HTML converter to write it as an @code{<aside>}.
}

@section{Code}

Code goes in a body written @code|{|{ ... }|}|, where an @"@" is just a
character, so nothing in it needs escaping:

@codeblock[#:lang "bjolang"]|{
  (defun (greet name)
    (str "Hello, " name "!"))

  ;; @em{this} is not a command here
}|

The block is indented with the text around it, and the indentation the lines
share is taken off.

@section{Lists, quotes and keys}

@itemlist{
  @item{Lists hold @strong{items},}
  @item{and an item may hold

    more than one paragraph.}
}

@itemlist[#:style ordered]{
  @item{Numbered,}
  @item{if you ask.}
}

Or write one the way Markdown does:

@read-list{
- quicker to type,
  - and nested by how far it is indented;
- with @em{commands} in it as anywhere else, like a reference
  back to @ref["code"].
}

@blockquote{
  "Quotes curl by themselves -- and so do dashes..."
}

Press @kbd{C-x C-s} to save, and mail linus@example.com if it breaks.

@section{Markdown too}

@markdown|{
Some things are *quicker* in Markdown, and it is translated into the same
tree as the rest of the post:

| Written in | Good for                         |
|:-----------|:---------------------------------|
| samizdat   | the whole post                   |
| Markdown   | ~~everything~~ lists and tables  |

- [x] footnotes,[^md] numbered with the rest
- [ ] a command inside, like |@kbd{C-c C-c}

[^md]: This one comes from Markdown.
}|
